  "slug": "sequencing-genome-dba8",
  "summary": "Spatial and network-level inference across cohorts and tumor types is now being tied to prognosis, survival, and altered gene programs.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational integration of molecular measurements to infer functional structure in tissue and disease. In ER+ breast cancer, network simulations and transcriptomics place phenotypic plasticity axes into opposing gene teams linked to survival. A prognostic tumor niche in 100 female genital tumors emerges from a Visium HD atlas and is predicted from H&E images by a POWER model. ONCOchannelome applies comparable logic to public RNA-seq from 15 tumor types, mapping altered ion channels and their coexpression networks. Other items apply integrative computation to spatial aging hotspots (stAge), a cross-etiological hepatic stellate cell atlas of 86,072 single-cell transcriptomes, and consensus NMF on Alzheimer's single-cell data. EVtrace, a SILAC proteomics pipeline for tracing extracellular vesicle proteins, is a methodological outlier. What is new is spatial and network-level inference applied across cohorts and tumor types, with prognostic relevance as the explicit endpoint in the clinical items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-30"
}
